{"name":"Breakaway Blue","hex":"#424d60","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"breakaway","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having broken away from a larger unit.","Capable of breaking off without damaging the larger structure."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of breaking away from something.","A group of riders which has gone ahead of the peloton."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breakaway"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Breakaway (Kelly Clarkson album)","blocks":[{"text":"Breakaway is the second studio album by American singer Kelly Clarkson, released on November 30, 2004, in Canada and in the United States, by RCA Records, with a wider release following throughout 2005. Despite the commercial success of Clarkson's debut album Thankful (2003), music critics still continued to typecast her as an American Idol winner and were also critical of her attempts of establishing a commercial appeal on her own. Wanting to stray from those, she collaborated with a range of new songwriters and producers such as Dr. Luke, Max Martin, John Shanks, Kara DioGuardi, and with former Evanescence members Ben Moody and David Hodges. This overhaul also led her to part ways with her manager Simon Fuller and hire the management services of Jeff Kwatinetz before the album's release."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/56/Breakaway_Album.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breakaway_(Kelly_Clarkson_album)","wikidata":"Q1026394"},{"title":"Breakaway (biscuit)","blocks":[{"text":"Breakaway was a brand of chocolate-covered digestive biscuit from Nestlé, which started production in 1970 in the United Kingdom, manufactured by Rowntree Mackintosh Confectionery. Nestlé acquired the brand in 1988. It was discontinued in February 2024 after 54 years due to falling sales."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Breakaway_%287%29.JPG/330px-Breakaway_%287%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breakaway_(biscuit)","wikidata":"Q4959379"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/38/Kelly_Clarkson_Breakaway_special_edition_cover.jpg/250px-Kelly_Clarkson_Breakaway_special_edition_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/38/Kelly_Clarkson_Breakaway_special_edition_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2